
=== Capture Health

The Capture Health screen shows whether changes were actively being captured for each configured table, month by month, over a rolling 13-month window.
Each row in the grid represents a table and each cell represents one calendar month.
The color of each cell indicates whether capture was active, whether the configuration changed, or whether a gap in coverage was detected.

image::images/analytics/analytics-capture-health.png[]

The color legend is displayed below the column headers.
*Active* (green) means capture was continuously active with no configuration changes or gaps during the month.
*Active (reconfigured)* (yellow) means the configuration changed one or more times during the month; the cell displays the single-letter reason code for the change, or "..." if there were multiple changes in the same month.
Clicking a yellow cell opens a dialog that lists each rebuild event and its reason.
*Partial — gap detected* (orange) means that capture was inactive for part of the month and some changes may not have been captured.
Clicking an orange cell opens a dialog showing the exact gap periods.
*No capture* (red) means that capture was inactive for the entire month.
*Not configured* (gray) means that capture had not yet been configured at that point in time.
For tables shown with a *No History* status, the configuration is in place but no capture history has been recorded yet; clicking any gray cell opens a dialog indicating when the configuration was created.

The summary cards at the top of the report show the total number of configured tables, the number currently capturing changes, the number not capturing, and the number with a capture gap detected in the current window.
Tables with issues are listed in a warning callout below the cards.
The grid is divided into three sections: *Not Capturing* (tables with a capture error or no history), *Gaps Detected*, and *Active — No Gaps Detected*.
Within each section, tables are ordered with the most recently configured first.
The *Capture Began* column shows the month and year that capture was first active, and the *Status* badge indicates the current capture state.
The *Reconfigs* column shows the total number of capture rebuilds over the 13-month window, and the *Gap Days* column shows the total number of days with no capture.

Use the *Schema* and *Catalog* filters to focus on a specific part of the schema hierarchy.
When both are available, selecting *All Catalogs* populates the Schema filter with schemas from every catalog; selecting a specific catalog limits the Schema filter to schemas within that catalog.
Use the *Channel* filter to show tables belonging to a specific channel.
Use the *Show* filter to restrict the grid to tables that are not currently capturing or to tables that are active.
Use the *Limit* filter to cap the number of rows shown.
Check *Show system tables* to include SymmetricDS internal tables in the report.
System tables are only shown when the SymmetricDS runtime tables are located in the same database as the application tables being replicated; if a separate runtime database is used, the checkbox is disabled.

Capture history is read from the `sym_trigger_hist` table.
The `purge.trigger.hist.retention.minutes` parameter controls how far back history is retained; the default value retains one year of history, which matches the 13-month window displayed by this report.
The retention duration is shown in the toolbar and can be edited from the *Data retained for* link.
